---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp/img/lab-1-25-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `ellipse(60, 200, 40, 40);
ellipse(140, 200, 40, 40);
ellipse(220, 200, 40, 40);`;
const Figure2 = `let xs = [60, 140, 220, 310];

for (const x of xs) {
  ellipse(x, 200, 40, 40);
}`;
const Figure3 = `let heights = [40, 120, 75, 160, 90];

for (let i = 0; i < heights.length; i++) {
  rect(i * 70 + 40, 300 - heights[i], 50, heights[i]);
}`;
const Figure4 = `let xs = [60, 140, 220];
let ys = [100, 250, 180];

for (let i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) {
  ellipse(xs[i], ys[i], 40, 40);
}`;
const Task1 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();
  fill(120, 200, 255);

  let xs = [60, 140, 220, 310];

  for (const x of xs) {
    ellipse(x, 200, 40, 40);
  }
}
`;
const Task2 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();
  fill(120, 200, 255);

  let xs = [60, 140, 220, 310];

  for (const x of xs) {
    ellipse(x, 200, 40, 40);
  }

  let heights = [40, 120, 75, 160, 90];

  for (let i = 0; i < heights.length; i++) {
    rect(i * 70 + 40, 380 - heights[i], 50, heights[i]);
  }
}
`;
const Task3 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();
  fill(120, 200, 255);

  let xs = [60, 140, 220, 310];

  for (const x of xs) {
    ellipse(x, 200, 40, 40);
  }

  let heights = [40, 120, 75, 160, 90];

  for (let i = 0; i < heights.length; i++) {
    rect(i * 70 + 40, 380 - heights[i], 50, heights[i]);
  }

  // Color each bar by its own height:
  //   red if the height is under 60
  //   yellow if it is under 120
  //   green otherwise
  //   remember fill() must come before the rect() it applies to
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.25: p5 Lab 6 &mdash; Drawing From Data</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />


        <!-- What You're Building -->
        <section id="target">
          <h2>What You're Building</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image src={TargetImage} alt="A dark canvas showing a row of blue circles above a bar chart of five blue bars of differing heights" width={400} class="img-fluid rounded" />
          </div>

          <p>By the end of <b>Task 2</b> both the circles and the bars will be drawn from arrays of numbers.</p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Task 3 is not shown.</b> That one is yours to work out.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />
        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.25</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">p5 Lab 6 &mdash; Drawing From Data</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>An Array of Positions</p>
              <p>A Chart Is a Traversal</p>
              <p>Parallel Arrays</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Three Circles, Three Lines</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Nothing wrong with it &mdash; until you want thirty, or until the
                positions come from somewhere else.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Data Moves Out of the Code</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Now the positions are <b class="text-sn-theme">data</b> and the
                drawing is <b class="text-sn-theme">code</b>. Change the array and
                the picture changes, without touching a single{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="ellipse()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>This Is the Whole Point</h2>
              <p>
                Grids from Lab 5 could only ever be regular, because the positions
                came from arithmetic.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                An array can hold <i>anything</i> &mdash; measurements, scores,
                positions that follow no pattern at all. The loop does not care.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>A Bar Chart</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Reading That Rectangle</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet code="i * 70 + 40" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> spaces the bars out &mdash; the
                    grid formula from Lab 5
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet code="heights[i]" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> is the bar's height, straight from
                    the data
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet code="300 - heights[i]" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> is where its <i>top</i> goes
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <p class="fragment">
                That subtraction is there because <b>y grows downward</b>. A
                taller bar needs a <i>smaller</i> y to reach further up. Lab 1's
                coordinate lesson, still earning its keep.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why an Index Here</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="for...of" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> would give you each height, but not{" "}
                <i>which</i> bar it is &mdash; and without that you cannot work
                out where to draw it.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                This is exactly the choice from Activity 1.24: need the position,
                use an index.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Parallel Arrays</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Two arrays, one index. <CodeSnippet code="xs[1]" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="ys[1]" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> describe the same circle.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Parallel Arrays</h2>
              <p>
                It works, and it is fragile: the two arrays must stay the same
                length and the same order, and nothing enforces that.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Real programs eventually use something better. For now, know the
                pattern and know its weakness &mdash; the exam asks about exactly
                this.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Drawing one shape per array element</li>
                <li>Using an element's value as a size or position</li>
                <li>Choosing an indexed loop when position matters</li>
                <li>Using two arrays together, and naming the risk</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Data-driven drawing</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A picture whose shape comes from values, not from hard-coded
                    numbers.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Parallel arrays</dt>
                  <dd>
                    Two or more arrays where the same index refers to the same
                    thing.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Visualization</dt>
                  <dd>Representing data so a person can see it at a glance.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js</b> sketch named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-25-data-drawing</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Circles From an Array</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it. Four circles, unevenly spaced.</li>
            <li>
              Add a fifth number to <CodeSnippet code="xs" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and run again{" "}
              <b>without touching the loop</b>. A fifth circle appears.
            </li>
            <li>
              Now remove two numbers. The drawing follows the data every time.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 6 — Drawing From Data"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: A Bar Chart</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a heights array and draw a bar for each value.</li>
            <li>
              All five bars should sit on the same baseline at y = 380 and grow
              upward by different amounts.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Try this:</b> change <CodeSnippet code="380 - heights[i]" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> to just{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="380" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and run. The bars grow the wrong way and fall
              off the canvas. Change it back.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 6 — Drawing From Data"
            ins={{ range: "13-17" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Color by Value</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The comments describe coloring each bar by its own height &mdash; a
              chain of conditions inside the traversal.
            </li>
            <li>
              This is Activity 1.13's status panel again, except the thresholds
              now come from array elements rather than separate variables.
            </li>
            <li>
              Order your chain carefully. Activity 1.12's rule applies exactly.
            </li>
            <li>
              Test by editing the heights so you get at least one bar of each
              color.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 6 — Drawing From Data"
            ins={{ range: "18-23" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): Label the Bars</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a second array of labels, one per bar, and draw each name under
              its bar using <CodeSnippet code="text()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
            </li>
            <li>
              You will need <CodeSnippet code="fill()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="textSize()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> before the text, or it will inherit the
              bar's color.
            </li>
            <li>
              This is parallel arrays for real. Add a label but forget the
              matching height, and note what happens &mdash; that is the fragility
              from the slides.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your finished sketch should show all of the following.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A row of circles whose positions come from an array.</li>
            <li>Five bars of different heights, all sitting on one baseline.</li>
            <li>Each bar colored by its own height, with at least two colors visible.</li>
            <li>
              <b>The real test:</b> adding a sixth height draws a sixth bar,
              correctly colored, with no other change to the code.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            If adding a value breaks the layout or the coloring, something in
            your loop is still counting to a fixed number.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Lab 5 drew a grid from arithmetic; this lab drew from an array.
              What can an array do that a formula cannot?
            </li>
            <li>
              The bar's y position is <code>380 - heights[i]</code> rather than
              just the height. Explain why, in terms of how the canvas is
              numbered.
            </li>
            <li>
              Parallel arrays rely on two lists staying in step. Describe a way
              that could go wrong, and what the drawing would look like when it
              did.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
